import {guide,section,diagram,choice,number,open} from './guide-author-engine.mjs';

guide('processes-threads-and-concurrency',{
 wordRange:[1300,1800],level:'beginner',minutes:60,
 summary:'Understand what actually runs inside a server: CPU work, processes, threads, waiting, and overlapping requests.',
 objectives:['Explain process, thread, concurrency and parallelism using one server.','Trace blocking and asynchronous waiting without assuming async means faster computation.','Identify a shared-state race and choose a bounded place for expensive work.'],
 terms:[['CPU core','A hardware execution resource that runs instructions; actual throughput depends on the work and processor.'],['Process','A running program with its own virtual address space and operating-system resources.'],['Thread','A schedulable execution path within a process; threads in that process share much of its memory.'],['Concurrency','Several tasks are in progress during overlapping periods.'],['Parallelism','Work executes at the same time on multiple execution resources.'],['Blocking','An execution path waits and cannot perform other work during that interval.'],['Event loop','A runtime mechanism that dispatches ready work and callbacks, often on one principal language execution thread.'],['Race condition','The result depends on the timing or ordering of competing operations.']],
 retrieval:['What work does the application server perform between receiving a request and replying?','Where does a saved note live after the server process restarts?'],
 sections:[
 section('machine','Zoom inside the server rectangle',`Your earlier diagram has a browser, a server, and a database. Now zoom inside the application server. It receives bytes, parses a request, checks a user, waits for data, formats a response, and sends bytes back. Some of these steps use the CPU actively. Others mostly wait for storage, another machine, or a network connection. This difference determines how the server can handle another request meanwhile.

A CPU executes instructions. Memory holds working data and program state; persistent storage keeps data beyond a process lifetime under its storage guarantees. Several CPU cores can do work in parallel, but adding cores does not automatically divide one sequential calculation among them. The program and runtime must expose work that can use those resources. For this guide, imagine an ordinary laptop running a small API. We are learning its behavior, not tuning an operating system or choosing a particular processor.`),
 section('process','A program file and a running process are different',`A program file is a recipe for behavior. Starting it creates a process with resources such as a virtual address space, open file or network handles, and an execution context. Two instances of the same API program can run as separate processes. Each normally has its own application memory. Assigning a variable in process A does not update the corresponding variable in process B.

That explains an important scaling surprise. If an API stores a user's shopping basket only in a process-local dictionary, a later request routed to another process may not find it. Shared access needs an explicit mechanism: a database, a cache service, shared memory with coordination, or messages between processes. A process boundary offers useful separation but does not automatically provide durability. When the process exits, its ordinary in-memory dictionary disappears. A server machine can run multiple processes, and one service can span multiple machines.`),
 section('thread','Threads share a room and need rules for shared objects',`Within a process, threads provide separate paths of execution. They share the process's address space and many resources while keeping execution state such as stacks. Think of two workers sharing a workshop: they can use the same shelves, so one can see objects another placed there. That convenience creates coordination responsibilities when both update the same object.

A thread pool is a bounded set of workers that receive tasks. A process pool uses separate process instances, which may offer stronger isolation but usually requires explicit communication and extra memory. Runtime details matter: different languages and implementations have different rules for executing language code on multiple cores. Do not assume that adding ten threads makes every Python, JavaScript, or Java program ten times faster. Ask which work is parallelizable, which runtime executes it, and whether the bottleneck is computation or waiting.`),
 section('overlap','Concurrency is about progress; parallelism is about simultaneous execution',`Imagine one cook preparing two dishes. While soup simmers without attention, the cook chops vegetables for the second dish. Both dishes are in progress, but the cook is actively doing one manual task at a time. This illustrates concurrency. Two cooks chopping independently at the same moment illustrate parallelism. The analogy has limits, but it separates the two questions clearly.

A single execution thread can manage many concurrent network requests if it yields while waiting and later resumes ready work. Multiple cores can execute independent computation in parallel if the program uses an appropriate execution model. A system can have both. Neither promises unlimited throughput: requests still consume memory, connections, CPU and downstream capacity. Concurrency is not a synonym for speed. Sometimes it improves resource use; sometimes too much concurrent work increases contention and makes every response slower.`),
 section('waiting','Work through a blocking request timeline',`Suppose a handler performs 10 ms of CPU work, waits 90 ms for a database response, then uses 5 ms of CPU to finish. These are invented measurements for a simple example. One request takes about 105 ms when it runs alone. If a single worker blocks through the entire database wait, a second request cannot start on that worker until the first finishes. Two requests then take roughly 210 ms in total, ignoring overhead.

With nonblocking database I/O, the first handler does its initial CPU work from 0 to 10 ms and starts the database request. The execution thread can start the second handler from 10 to 20 ms and initiate its database request. If each database operation independently waits 90 ms, the first result is ready at 100 ms and the second at 110 ms. Their 5 ms finishing steps can complete around 105 and 115 ms. We overlapped waiting; we did not make either database operation intrinsically faster.`),
 section('event-loop','An event loop helps only when callbacks give it time',`An event-driven runtime dispatches ready callbacks or tasks. When supported asynchronous I/O starts, the runtime arranges to learn when it is ready, and the application can return control instead of waiting on the same execution thread. The runtime may use operating-system readiness mechanisms or worker threads for particular operations; “asynchronous” does not mean every operation uses the same mechanism.

In a typical Node.js application, long-running JavaScript on the main event-loop thread delays other JavaScript callbacks on that thread. Marking a function async or wrapping a calculation in a Promise does not move that calculation to another CPU. A 500 ms synchronous loop can still delay a small health response queued behind it. Move suitable CPU-heavy work to explicitly managed workers or processes, divide it into bounded cooperative pieces when appropriate, or place it behind an asynchronous job service. Each choice needs limits and error handling.`),
 section('cpu','Distinguish waiting capacity from computation capacity',`Assume a calculation needs 20 ms of CPU time on one execution resource under a measured workload. One such resource has an ideal upper bound of about fifty calculations per second because 1,000 ms divided by 20 ms is fifty. Real overhead reduces that bound. Starting a thousand concurrent calculations does not create more CPU time; it creates waiting, memory pressure, or scheduling overhead.

By contrast, an I/O-heavy service may keep the CPU useful by overlapping many waits. That still has a limit: a database connection pool of twenty cannot give every one of ten thousand requests a simultaneous connection. Place bounds on in-flight requests, queued work, and worker count. Measure CPU utilization, queue delay, memory use and downstream waiting separately. A busy CPU suggests one investigation; low CPU with long connection waits suggests another. Do not choose more threads until you know which resource is limiting useful work.`),
 section('race','A tiny shared-state example exposes the danger',`There is one available study-session seat. Request A reads available = 1. Before A updates it, request B also reads 1. A accepts a booking and writes 0; B accepts another booking and writes 0. The final counter looks plausible, yet two bookings were accepted for one seat. Each request's individual instructions were ordinary; their interleaving violated the business rule.

Protect the whole check-and-reserve decision at the authoritative state boundary. Inside one process that may involve a lock or another synchronization primitive. Across API processes, a process-local lock is insufficient: use a database transaction, atomic conditional update, or another shared concurrency mechanism with the required guarantee. Avoid holding scarce local locks or database connections through unnecessary remote waits. Even a single-threaded event loop can have logical races when a function yields between reading state and using that stale state later.`),
 section('design','Apply the distinction to a small AI feature',`Our notes app adds “summarize this document.” Parsing a huge document synchronously on the same event-loop thread can delay unrelated note reads. Sending a remote model request is primarily a wait from the API's perspective, but each in-flight request still holds state and may consume provider quota. If a local model runs, its computation uses separate CPU or GPU resources whose capacity must also be bounded.

A reasonable first design validates a bounded request, records a job, and returns a job identity. A bounded worker pool performs expensive parsing and summarization, then saves a result the client can retrieve. This adds a queue and status lifecycle, so use it when the latency and workload justify it. For a tiny quick operation, a simpler synchronous response may be fine. Explain the resource being protected, the waiting limit, and the failure behavior. Those are the design skills these operating-system words are meant to support.`),
 ],
 diagrams:[diagram('process-memory','process','One machine can contain independent application processes','flowchart TB\nM[Server machine with CPU and memory] --> A[API process A]\nM --> B[API process B]\nA --> TA[Threads share memory inside A]\nB --> TB[Threads share memory inside B]\nA --> D[(Explicit shared database)]\nB --> D', ['Starting the program twice creates separate process instances.','Threads within an instance share its application memory.','Cross-process state requires an explicit shared mechanism.']),diagram('overlap-waits','waiting','The execution thread can use another request’s waiting time','sequenceDiagram\nparticipant E as Execution thread\nparticipant D as Database\nE->>D: A starts query after 10 ms CPU\nNote over E: Start B using another 10 ms CPU\nE->>D: B starts query\nD-->>E: A result ready around 100 ms\nNote over E: Finish A in 5 ms\nD-->>E: B result ready around 110 ms\nNote over E: Finish B in 5 ms', ['A begins I/O and yields.','B uses the thread while A is waiting.','Ready responses resume bounded CPU work.'], 'Illustrative independent database waits; real capacity, scheduling, and contention can change these timings.')],
 exercise:{minutes:15,prompt:'On paper, trace two requests that each need 10 ms CPU, 90 ms independent I/O wait, and 5 ms CPU. Compare one fully blocking worker with nonblocking waiting. Then explain what changes if each request instead needs 100 ms of uninterrupted CPU. Finally trace two callers competing for one remaining seat.',rubric:['Separate response duration from CPU time.','Overlap waits without claiming the database became faster.','Do not assume async creates CPU parallelism.','Protect the whole seat check and reservation.'],solution:'Fully blocking execution takes roughly 210 ms for two requests. Under the stated independent-I/O timeline, nonblocking handling can finish them around 105 and 115 ms. If each requires 100 ms of uninterrupted CPU on one execution thread, the work still needs about 200 ms total before overhead; async syntax alone does not parallelize it. The seat race permits both callers to read one and both accept. Use a transaction or atomic conditional reservation at the shared state authority; a local lock does not coordinate separate processes.'},
 questions:[choice('q1','Which pair normally shares one process’s address space?',['Two threads in the same process','Any two processes on the same host','Two unrelated machines'],'Two threads in the same process','Threads within a process share much of its memory, which makes synchronization important.'),number('q2','A calculation needs 20 ms CPU on one execution resource. Ideal calculations/s before overhead?',50,'calculations/s','1,000 ÷ 20 = 50; concurrency alone does not raise this bound.'),choice('q3','Does adding async to a CPU-heavy function automatically move it off the event-loop thread?',['No','Yes, always','Only when the function has a long name'],'No','Execution moves only through an explicit mechanism or runtime behavior that actually schedules it elsewhere.'),open('q4','Why can a single-threaded event loop still have a logical lost-update race?','A handler can read state, yield for I/O, then resume after another handler changed it. The earlier read is stale; protect the full authoritative decision.'),open('q5','When would you move summarization into a bounded worker job?','When expensive or long-running processing would exceed request deadlines or monopolize request-serving resources. Persist job state, cap concurrency/queue age, return a job identity and describe retries and results.')],
 flashcards:[['Process versus program file?','A process is a running instance with its own resources; the file describes the program.'],['Thread memory boundary?','Threads share their process’s memory and need rules for concurrent updates.'],['Concurrency versus parallelism?','Overlapping progress versus simultaneous execution on multiple resources.'],['Async I/O improves what?','It can overlap waiting with other work; it does not itself speed CPU computation.'],['Shared check-and-update rule?','The complete decision must be protected at the authoritative shared state boundary.']],
 mentalModel:'Ask whether a request is using a processor, waiting for something else, or waiting its turn—and never assume more tasks create more resources.',sources:[['Microsoft — Processes and threads','https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/procthread/about-processes-and-threads'],['Node.js — Do not block the event loop or worker pool','https://nodejs.org/en/learn/asynchronous-work/dont-block-the-event-loop']]
});

guide('queue-broker-fundamentals',{
 wordRange:[1300,1800],level:'intermediate',minutes:60,
 summary:'Choose messaging behavior before choosing a broker: competing workers, independent subscribers, retained logs, acknowledgements, and replay.',
 objectives:['Distinguish a work queue, publish/subscribe routing, and a retained event log.','Compare RabbitMQ, SQS, Kafka, Redis Pub/Sub and Redis Streams by required behavior.','Trace a durable AI job across publication, processing, retry and acknowledgement failures.'],
 terms:[['Producer','A program that publishes work or events.'],['Consumer','A program that receives and processes published work or events.'],['Broker','Software or a managed service that mediates message storage, routing or delivery.'],['Acknowledgement','A signal that a specifically defined delivery or processing step completed.'],['Visibility timeout','A period during which a received SQS message is hidden from ordinary competing receives.'],['Offset','A position within a retained log partition.'],['Consumer group','A coordination unit whose members share consumption work under the system’s rules.'],['Replay','Reading retained events again from an earlier position.']],
 retrieval:['What does Redis do when used as a cache, and why is that a separate role from durable job storage?','Why should expensive document processing have a bounded amount of concurrent work?'],
 sections:[
 section('why','A queue changes when the work happens',`A learner uploads a document and asks for an AI summary. Parsing and inference may take longer than a comfortable interactive request. The API can accept a bounded job, save its identity and status, and arrange for a worker to process it later. The browser receives a job ID and can return to useful work while it waits. This changes the interface from “here is the finished summary” to “your job was accepted; here is how to observe it.”

The queue is a waiting mechanism between producers and consumers. It can absorb a temporary burst when workers are busy, but it cannot make permanently insufficient capacity sufficient. If twenty jobs arrive per second while workers complete ten, waiting work grows by ten jobs each second. Set an admission policy, maximum wait, and failure response. Otherwise “asynchronous” becomes a polite label for an ever-growing delay.`),
 section('patterns','Separate three messaging questions',`A work queue asks: which available worker should process this task? Competing workers share the load, and each task is intended to have one logical effect even though attempts may repeat. Publish/subscribe asks: which interested applications should receive this event? A document-uploaded event may interest indexing, summarization and audit consumers independently. Each application needs its own intended delivery path.

A retained log asks: where should events be stored in order so consumers can track positions and read them again? One application may be caught up while another is hours behind. Reading an event does not inherently delete it. These ideas can be combined: a topic can route copies into several work queues, and members of one consumer group can share a log’s partitions. Avoid treating the product words queue, topic and stream as interchangeable guarantees. Define delivery ownership, retention and replay explicitly.`),
 section('ack','There are two different acknowledgement boundaries',`A publisher wants evidence that the broker accepted its publication under the configured durability rules. A worker wants to tell the broker that it has finished handling a delivery. Those are different acknowledgements. A broker accepting a job does not prove the worker produced a summary, and a worker receiving bytes does not prove its result was saved.

Consider a crash after the worker saves the result but before it acknowledges the delivery. The broker may redeliver, so the next attempt must recognize that the job already has a valid result. Acknowledging before saving creates the opposite risk: a crash can lose the only opportunity to finish the work. Durable state plus idempotent processing makes the repeated-attempt boundary manageable. External model calls or other side effects may need additional provider-specific identities or reconciliation; a broker label cannot promise exactly-once effects everywhere.`),
 section('rabbit','RabbitMQ: route messages into consumable queues',`RabbitMQ can route publications through exchanges to queues using configured bindings. This is useful when applications need explicit routing and work distribution. In the common queue model, consumers acknowledge processed deliveries, and unacknowledged work can be redelivered after the relevant consumer connection or channel fails. Publisher confirms are a separate mechanism for publication evidence. Configure the queue type, durability and message persistence according to the required failure tolerance.

Use a prefetch or equivalent in-flight limit so one consumer does not hold far more uncompleted work than it can process. A repeated negative acknowledgement without delay can create a hot retry loop rather than recovery. RabbitMQ also offers streams and other capabilities, so do not reduce the entire product to one queue abstraction. For a first job system, explain the chosen queue semantics and failure configuration before claiming that installing the broker made every message durable.`),
 section('sqs','SQS: managed queue delivery with a visibility lease',`Amazon SQS provides managed queues. After a receive, a message is normally hidden for a visibility timeout while the worker handles it. The worker deletes it after successful handling. If it is not deleted before the visibility period expires, it becomes eligible for another receive. A long-running job may need a bounded visibility extension policy, but extending invisibility is not the same as committing a business result.

Standard queues permit duplicate delivery and do not promise strict ordering. FIFO queues provide different ordering and deduplication features with their own scope and limits; they still do not make arbitrary downstream side effects transactional. Choose based on required behavior, throughput, operations and environment. For our AI example, if a ten-minute job has a thirty-second visibility period with no extension, concurrent repeated processing is predictable. Match the lease policy to observed work, retain a stable job identity, and handle worker loss explicitly.`),
 section('kafka','Kafka: retained partitioned history and independent progress',`Kafka organizes retained records into topic partitions. A partition is an ordered sequence, and consumer progress is represented by offsets. Consumers can read retained records again rather than deleting each record when it is processed. Independent applications can maintain independent progress, while members of a normal consumer group distribute assigned partitions. Retention and compaction policies determine which history remains available; a log is not automatically an eternal archive.

This fits a stream of document lifecycle events that indexing, analytics and audit applications need to consume independently or reprocess. Ordering is scoped to a partition, not an automatic global order over the topic. The key and partitioning choice therefore matter. A consumer committing an offset before its result is durable can skip work after a crash; committing afterward can repeat work. Kafka has transaction capabilities for defined Kafka workflows, but writing an external database or calling an external AI provider still requires its own end-to-end reasoning.`),
 section('redis','Redis messaging is more than one feature',`Redis Pub/Sub distributes messages to currently subscribed clients. Its documented delivery is at most once: a disconnected subscriber does not later retrieve missed publications from Pub/Sub history. That can fit disposable live hints, such as “refresh this view,” when the client can read authoritative state afterward. It is a poor sole durable record for a paid summary job that must survive consumer downtime.

Redis Streams is a different data structure with retained entries, IDs, consumer-group features and pending-message handling. It can support replay and coordinated work patterns when persistence, replication, retention and recovery are configured appropriately. A Redis cache entry, a Pub/Sub publication and a stream record are three distinct choices. Sharing the Redis product name does not give them identical guarantees. If cache eviction policies might remove job state, examine that interaction instead of assuming all data in Redis has the same acceptable loss policy.`),
 section('worked','Trace the AI job through a complete durable flow',`The API authenticates the user, verifies document access, assigns job J17 and saves pending status plus publication intent atomically in its database. A publisher sends the durable job reference to the chosen queue; duplicate publication remains possible. A worker receives J17, checks whether a final result already exists, claims work through a defined concurrency rule, and loads only the authorized document reference. Keep large document bytes out of the message unless there is a specific reason to carry them there.

The worker performs bounded parsing and an allowed model call, then saves the result and completed status before acknowledging or deleting the delivery. A crash after save is recovered by recognizing the completed job. A crash during a model call may leave an uncertain charged operation, so choose a retry/reconciliation policy that limits duplicate cost. The browser reads status from the durable job store. A transient progress publication can improve responsiveness without becoming the sole evidence that the job exists.`),
 section('operate','Choose from the failure questions, then operate the backlog',`Ask whether each consumer needs every event, whether replay is required, what ordering scope matters, and how long data must remain. Also ask who operates the broker, where the service runs, and which delivery states need inspection. A small managed work queue may suit independent AI jobs; a retained partitioned log may suit several applications replaying lifecycle events. Product choice follows those requirements rather than a universal ranking of technologies.

Observe oldest-job age, arrival and completion rates, in-flight count, retries, dead-letter reasons and downstream quota use. Send permanently invalid inputs to an explicit failed state; delay transient retries with a budget instead of immediately looping. Replaying dead-letter work should preserve its logical identity and be reviewable. Test duplicate delivery, consumer crash before and after result save, publication failure, a slow model provider and expired credentials. A reliable queue-based application is the complete state machine around the broker, not just the broker itself.`),
 ],
 diagrams:[diagram('messaging-patterns','patterns','Three patterns answer different questions','flowchart TB\nP[Producer] --> Q[Work queue]\nQ --> W1[Competing worker A]\nQ --> W2[Competing worker B]\nE[Event publisher] --> T[Pub-sub routing]\nT --> I[Indexing subscription]\nT --> A[Audit subscription]\nL[(Retained partition log)] --> C1[Consumer with cursor 20]\nL --> C2[Consumer with cursor 12]', ['Work queues distribute task attempts among workers.','Pub/sub gives independent applications their own event delivery.','A retained log allows different cursors and replay.'], 'These patterns can be combined. Arrows do not claim every product implements every delivery guarantee identically.'),diagram('durable-ai-job','worked','The durable job store defines accepted and completed','flowchart LR\nU[Submit authorized document] --> A[API]\nA --> D[(Job and publication intent)]\nD --> P[Publisher]\nP --> Q[Chosen broker]\nQ --> W[Bounded worker]\nW --> M[Model call]\nW --> R[(Result and completed status)]\nR --> K[Acknowledge delivery]\nU --> S[Read durable job status]', ['Persist the job before claiming acceptance.','Publish with a stable job identity.','Save the completed result before acknowledging work.','Treat uncertain external calls separately from broker delivery.'])],
 exercise:{minutes:15,prompt:'Pick a messaging pattern for: distributing summary jobs, sending upload events independently to indexing and audit, and reprocessing a week of retained lifecycle events. Then trace J17 when the worker saves its result and crashes before acknowledging. Explain why Redis Pub/Sub alone does not preserve the job.',rubric:['Differentiate competing work, independent delivery, and retained replay.','Acknowledge only after the chosen durable result boundary.','Recognize repeated work with stable job identity.','Explain Pub/Sub loss during subscriber downtime.'],solution:'Summary attempts fit a work queue. Indexing and audit need independent subscriptions or consumer groups. Reprocessing needs retained history and a cursor/reset policy, such as a suitably configured log. After J17 is saved and the acknowledgement is lost, redelivery checks the durable completed result and returns/acknowledges without repeating the logical effect. Redis Pub/Sub has no stored replay for a disconnected subscriber; retain the job in a durable store and use a queue/stream configuration matching the recovery requirement. An external paid call may still need provider-specific duplicate-cost handling.'},
 questions:[choice('q1','A publisher confirmation proves which boundary?',['The broker accepted publication under its configured rules','The user read the finished result','Every external side effect happened exactly once'],'The broker accepted publication under its configured rules','Publication evidence and completed consumer work are distinct.'),number('q2','Twenty jobs/s arrive and ten jobs/s finish. Backlog growth?',10,'jobs/s','20 − 10 = 10 jobs/s; a queue cannot fix a sustained capacity deficit.'),choice('q3','Which Redis feature is suitable for retained-entry replay when correctly configured?',['Redis Streams','Redis Pub/Sub alone','An evictable temporary cache key with no policy'],'Redis Streams','Streams retains entries and supports consumption state; durability and retention still require configuration.'),open('q4','What happens when SQS visibility expires while a worker is still computing?','The message becomes eligible for another receive, so overlapping repeated work can occur. Use suitable bounded extensions and a stable authoritative job/claim protocol; do not rely on invisibility as a permanent lock.'),open('q5','Why does Kafka partition ordering not give all records one global order?','Each partition has its own ordered log. Choosing keys and partition mapping scopes the ordering; consuming several partitions introduces separate sequences and processing schedules.')],
 flashcards:[['Work queue question?','Which available worker processes this task attempt?'],['Pub/sub question?','Which independent interested applications should receive the event?'],['Retained log question?','Where is ordered history kept so consumers can track positions and replay?'],['Result-save before acknowledgement creates what crash case?','Repeated delivery after a saved result; recognize it with stable identity and idempotent handling.'],['Queue health beyond length?','Oldest age, arrival/completion rate, in-flight work, retries and downstream capacity.']],
 mentalModel:'Choose who should see the message, how long it survives, and what acknowledgement proves—then choose the broker.',sources:[['RabbitMQ — Consumer acknowledgements and publisher confirms','https://www.rabbitmq.com/docs/confirms'],['Amazon SQS — Visibility timeout','https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AWSSimpleQueueService/latest/SQSDeveloperGuide/sqs-visibility-timeout.html'],['Apache Kafka — Introduction and main concepts','https://kafka.apache.org/42/getting-started/introduction/'],['Redis — Pub/Sub delivery semantics','https://redis.io/docs/latest/develop/pubsub/'],['Redis — Streams','https://redis.io/docs/latest/develop/data-types/streams/'],['AWS — Transactional outbox pattern','https://docs.aws.amazon.com/prescriptive-guidance/latest/cloud-design-patterns/transactional-outbox.html']]
});

guide('service-boundaries',{
 wordRange:[1300,1800],level:'beginner',minutes:60,
 summary:'Learn when a module should stay a local call and when a separate service earns the extra network and operational complexity.',
 objectives:['Distinguish a modular codebase from independently deployed services.','Explain what changes when a local call becomes remote.','Choose a service boundary using ownership, workload, failure behavior and team needs.'],
 terms:[['Monolith','An application deployed as one main unit; its internal code can still be well organized.'],['Module','A code boundary that groups a coherent responsibility behind an interface.'],['Service','An independently running component reached through a defined communication contract.'],['Coupling','How strongly a change or failure in one component forces a change or failure elsewhere.'],['Data ownership','Responsibility for a fact’s meaning, allowed updates and authoritative representation.'],['Partial failure','Some participating components work while another component or network path fails.'],['Deployment boundary','A unit that can be released, scaled or restarted as part of an operational action.']],
 retrieval:['Why do separate processes not automatically share their in-memory dictionaries?','What remains uncertain when a remote write times out?'],
 sections:[
 section('one-app','A useful application can start as one deployable program',`Imagine a small learning portal with accounts, notes, document uploads, and document summaries. One application handles its API and uses one relational database. A team can run it locally, deploy a new version, inspect one set of application logs, and follow a request without crossing several internal networks. This is a reasonable beginning if it meets the workload and organizational needs.

Calling it a monolith says something about deployment, not necessarily code quality. It may be carefully organized or tangled. It may run as one instance or as multiple instances behind a load balancer. Horizontal replication of a monolithic API can work well when requests do not depend on unsafely isolated process-local state. Conversely, dividing a tangled design into ten network services does not automatically remove the tangled dependencies; it can make them harder to observe and change.`),
 section('modules','Create clear responsibilities before creating network hops',`Inside the portal, define modules for note storage, document metadata, summary requests, and user preferences. Each module exposes a small interface and keeps its implementation details private. The note module might offer createNote and findAuthorizedNote rather than inviting every other module to modify its tables directly. A module can call another module in the same process through an ordinary function interface.

This organization is often called a modular monolith when the modules share one deployment. It gives a learner a practical way to practice boundaries without immediately adding distributed-system failure modes. State which module owns each rule: document visibility belongs with document access, while summary-job status belongs with summary processing. Separate names help only if callers respect the interface. If every module edits every table, the ownership remains unclear even when the folders have attractive names.`),
 section('remote','A remote call changes the failure model',`A local function call and a network request may look similarly simple in source code, especially when a client library hides the request. Their failure models differ. A remote request needs serialization, addressing, connection handling and a response contract. It can fail because a process is down, a route is unavailable, the connection is slow, or the response is lost after the remote work succeeds.

Set a deadline and distinguish retry-safe reads from writes that need stable operation identities. Cancellation can stop local waiting without proving remote work stopped. Every extra synchronous dependency is another place the current operation may wait. This does not make remote services bad; it makes their cost concrete. If three sequential remote calls each add 50 ms of observed round-trip overhead under an example workload, they add roughly 150 ms before their other processing costs. Independent work may overlap, but dependent work cannot simply be declared parallel.`),
 section('ownership','Keep one authoritative owner for each fact',`In the original portal, creating a document record and a summary request can use one local database transaction when the application owns both updates. If summary processing becomes a separate service with a separate database, one ordinary transaction no longer automatically covers both stores. The first update can commit while the second fails. A design must expose or repair that intermediate state.

Owning data means owning its permitted changes, not necessarily buying a separate database server for every table on day one. Within a modular application, table access rules can establish logical ownership. Across services, prefer a defined API or event contract to unrestricted writes into another service’s private tables. Shared direct writes bind deployments to the same schema and make independent evolution difficult. Some reporting copies are useful, but label them as derived and specify their freshness rather than treating two stores as equal authorities.`),
 section('failure','Isolation requires behavior, not only separate processes',`Suppose the portal calls a remote recommendation service before returning every note. Moving recommendations into a separate process may isolate a crash, but if the note API waits indefinitely for it, users still experience a notes outage. Fault isolation requires bounded waiting and an acceptable fallback, such as returning the note without optional suggestions. Process separation is one tool; dependency policy determines the user outcome.

Resource isolation also needs explicit budgets. A summary service can fill a shared database connection pool or saturate a network link even when it has its own container. Limit concurrent work, message sizes and queue growth. Monitor the dependency from the caller’s perspective. A service reporting healthy does not establish that its caller can reach it with the required identity and deadline. Start from the user journey and identify which dependencies are essential and which may degrade.`),
 section('split','Give the split a reason that can be evaluated',`A separate summary worker may be justified because document parsing and model execution have a different resource profile from reading small notes. It may need different deployment timing, a specialized runtime, or stricter limits around untrusted files. An independently owned business capability with a stable interface can also justify a service boundary when separate teams need to release changes without coordinating every deployment.

Costs remain: more builds and releases, configuration, credentials, telemetry, integration tests, failure handling and version compatibility. A three-person team supporting a modest portal may benefit from keeping most modules together and extracting only expensive work. A larger organization may make different choices. “We expect growth” alone is too vague. State the growth or operational pain, the proposed boundary, the benefit it enables, and evidence that its extra responsibilities are manageable.`),
 section('worked','Extract document summaries without breaking note reads',`Before extraction, a POST /summaries handler parses the document and waits for a model response inside the main API. During a burst, expensive work delays ordinary note reads. After extraction, the API authenticates the user, validates document access and size, records a durable job with an operation identity, and returns a job ID. A bounded worker retrieves eligible work, processes it, and saves a result and status.

The client reads job status or receives progress events. Note reads no longer wait for model completion. The worker still checks the relevant document authorization or an explicitly scoped internal grant; a job ID is not automatic permission to read private input. Define what happens after a worker crashes, how repeated execution recognizes an existing result, and how expired documents affect queued work. A durable queue or later outbox pattern can support the handoff, but merely drawing a queue does not prove the source update and publication are atomic.`),
 section('contracts','Independent releases need compatible interfaces and trust boundaries',`The summary worker version may change while the API stays unchanged. Version the job payload or maintain a compatible schema, validate fields, and define behavior for unsupported versions. Adding an optional field is often easier to roll out than changing the meaning of an existing required one. Test the combinations expected during a rolling deployment. Independence is reduced if every release requires all services to switch simultaneously.

Authenticate service-to-service callers and carry the minimum user or tenant context needed for authorization. Do not trust a tenant ID simply because it arrived from another internal IP address. Limit credentials to the responsibility of the service. In Kubernetes, a Service is also the name of a networking resource that provides a stable way to reach selected workloads. That capitalized platform object is not the same concept as a business service boundary; one does not automatically define the other.`),
 section('decision','Explain the choice without turning an architecture style into a goal',`For the learning portal, keep note and account modules in a coherent application initially, and move heavy summary execution behind a bounded job interface if measurements justify it. Keep durable job ownership and result semantics explicit. This is a specific design choice for a specific workload, not a rule that every application needs microservices or that no application should use them.

Review a proposed split with four questions. Which behavior and data does the component own? What can now be deployed or scaled independently? What happens when its call fails halfway through? What new operational work does the team accept? Draw the before and after request paths and test one failure. Write the expected user-visible state beside every failed arrow so hidden assumptions become reviewable. A good boundary makes a responsibility easier to understand and change. If the split creates many tiny synchronous calls with no independent benefit, reconsider the boundary before adding more infrastructure.`),
 ],
 diagrams:[diagram('modular-app','modules','Logical boundaries can exist inside one deployment','flowchart TB\nU[Portal client] --> A[One application deployment]\nA --> N[Notes module]\nA --> D[Documents module]\nA --> S[Summary module]\nN --> DB[(Owned tables in shared database)]\nD --> DB\nS --> DB', ['Modules group coherent rules and interfaces.','Calls can remain in the same process.','Table ownership is explicit even when infrastructure is shared.']),diagram('extract-summary','worked','Extract the expensive responsibility with a durable interface','flowchart LR\nU[Client] --> A[Portal API]\nA --> N[Fast note operations]\nA --> J[(Durable summary job)]\nJ --> W[Bounded summary worker]\nW --> M[Model service]\nW --> R[(Result and status)]\nA --> R', ['The API validates and records intent.','Workers use their own capacity budget.','Results become durable state that clients can read.','The ordinary note path avoids waiting for model execution.'])],
 exercise:{minutes:15,prompt:'A modular portal serves notes quickly until large document summaries arrive. Propose one justified extraction. Name the new API/job contract, data owner, retry boundary, and fallback when the worker is down. Then give one reason to keep the remaining modules in one deployment.',rubric:['Tie extraction to measured resource or release needs.','Persist work and status with explicit ownership.','Handle partial failure and duplicate execution.','Explain operational costs and what stays simpler.'],solution:'Extract summary execution behind a durable job interface with bounded input, operation ID, status and result. The portal owns document access; the job subsystem owns processing state under a defined schema. A down worker leaves accepted jobs pending within a documented age bound, while note reads remain available. Repeated attempts must not create duplicate logical results or repeated uncontrolled paid calls. Keep coherent low-cost modules together to preserve simple local transactions, testing and deployment until independent scaling or ownership requires more boundaries.'},
 questions:[choice('q1','What does “modular monolith” describe?',['Clear internal modules in one main deployment','A system with no interfaces','A rule forbidding multiple application instances'],'Clear internal modules in one main deployment','Code boundaries and deployment boundaries are different choices.'),number('q2','Three sequential remote calls each add 50 ms of observed round-trip overhead. Approximate added overhead?',150,'ms','3 × 50 = 150 ms before other processing costs.'),choice('q3','Which is a concrete reason to extract summary execution?',['Its workload needs a separately bounded specialized compute pool','Microservices are always better','A diagram looks empty'],'Its workload needs a separately bounded specialized compute pool','The separation supports an explicit workload and operational requirement.'),open('q4','Why does a separate recommendation process not automatically protect note availability?','If note reads synchronously wait for it without deadlines or fallback, its failure still blocks the user journey. Isolation requires dependency and resource policies.'),open('q5','What changes when one transaction’s updates move into two separately owned databases?','There is no automatic shared local transaction; either update may commit while the other fails. Model intermediate state, durable intent, idempotent retries, reconciliation or a suitable coordination protocol.')],
 flashcards:[['Module boundary?','A code responsibility and interface; it need not be a network service.'],['Deployment boundary?','A unit that is released, scaled or restarted operationally.'],['Remote-call cost?','Latency, serialization, versioning, identity and partial failure.'],['Data owner?','The component responsible for a fact’s meaning and permitted authoritative updates.'],['Good reason to split?','A specific independent workload, ownership, release or isolation need whose benefit outweighs operational cost.']],
 mentalModel:'Draw a boundary around a responsibility first; add a network boundary only when its benefit pays for the new failure modes.',sources:[['Microsoft Architecture Center — Microservices benefits and challenges','https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/architecture/guide/architecture-styles/microservices'],['AWS — Transactional outbox and the dual-write problem','https://docs.aws.amazon.com/prescriptive-guidance/latest/cloud-design-patterns/transactional-outbox.html'],['Google SRE — Handling overload','https://sre.google/sre-book/handling-overload/']]
});

guide('connection-and-protocol-basics',{
 wordRange:[1300,1800],level:'beginner',minutes:60,
 summary:'Follow a real connection through TCP or QUIC, encryption, HTTP messages, and application contracts without treating every protocol as a competitor.',
 objectives:['Explain what TCP, UDP, TLS and HTTP each contribute.','Compare HTTP versions without confusing multiplexing with unlimited capacity.','Choose ordinary HTTP, gRPC, WebSocket or server-sent events for a concrete communication pattern.'],
 terms:[['Protocol','Rules that communicating programs follow to exchange and interpret messages.'],['Connection','An established communication relationship with state at its endpoints; exact semantics depend on the protocol.'],['TCP','A transport providing a reliable ordered byte stream between endpoints.'],['UDP','A transport sending datagrams without TCP-style reliability or ordering guarantees.'],['TLS','A protocol providing protected communication with authentication and confidentiality under its security assumptions.'],['Multiplexing','Carrying multiple logical streams through one underlying connection.'],['gRPC','A framework for calling defined remote procedures, commonly using Protocol Buffers and HTTP/2.'],['SSE','Server-sent events: a server-to-client event stream carried over HTTP.']],
 retrieval:['Why do the client and server need agreed request and response formats?','What does a timeout tell you about whether a remote write committed?'],
 sections:[
 section('layers','Begin with different jobs, not a list of acronyms',`When the notes browser asks for note 42, several sets of rules cooperate. An address helps locate the destination. A transport carries data between endpoints. A security layer protects communication. An application protocol describes messages such as requests and responses. The application itself decides whether the user may read note 42 and what its JSON fields mean.

These are different responsibilities. Comparing TLS with HTTP as if you must choose one misses how they work together. Likewise, gRPC is not a replacement for every network layer; it uses underlying communication mechanisms. You do not need to memorize the entire OSI model to follow this request. Ask four concrete questions: where do the bytes go, what delivery behavior is provided, who can read or impersonate the endpoint, and how does the application interpret the result? Each question has a different owner.`),
 section('tcp','TCP provides an ordered stream, not a business transaction',`TCP establishes a connection and delivers bytes in order, repairing loss through its transport mechanisms while managing flow and congestion. The receiving application reads a stream, so a single application message may arrive through several reads, and several messages may be available together. Applications need framing rules to determine where their messages begin and end. HTTP supplies its own message and stream structure above the transport.

TCP reliability does not mean an application action occurs exactly once. The server can commit a save and lose the response before the client sees it. A new connection and retry can submit the operation again. The business layer still needs an idempotency identity or reconciliation rule. Nor does an established TCP connection prove that the server is healthy enough to answer promptly. It can remain connected while a dependency is blocked or the application has no capacity.`),
 section('udp','UDP leaves more work to the protocol above it',`UDP sends datagrams, preserving message boundaries, without promising reliable ordered delivery like TCP. Datagrams may be lost, duplicated or reordered. That does not mean every UDP-based application must accept arbitrary corruption or poor behavior. A higher-level protocol can add authentication, reliability, congestion control, or recovery suited to its needs.

For a live audio application, late data may be less useful than newer data, so its policy can differ from transferring a database backup. QUIC is another important example: it runs over UDP while implementing secure connections and reliable streams itself. Therefore “UDP means no reliability” is only accurate as a statement about the UDP layer’s guarantees, not every application built above it. Choose according to the complete stack and user requirement. A beginner should explain the distinction before making a blanket claim that UDP is always faster or TCP is always better.`),
 section('tls','HTTPS protects a hop; authorization protects an action',`In a typical HTTPS connection, TLS protects the communication and authenticates the server using a certificate validated for the intended name and trust chain. Do not disable certificate validation to make a connection work. TLS helps prevent a network observer from reading or modifying protected application content, but it does not make a malicious server honest or grant a user permission to read another user’s note.

A reverse proxy may terminate the browser’s TLS connection and create a separate connection to the application. Draw those as two hops and state whether the internal hop is also protected. Calling the whole path encrypted requires checking every relevant hop. Some metadata, such as network endpoints, can remain observable; encryption is not a claim that no information about communication exists. Authentication identifies a principal, and authorization checks which action that principal may perform. Those checks still belong in the service architecture.`),
 section('http','HTTP gives the interaction meaning',`An HTTP request includes a method, target, headers, and sometimes a body. A response carries a status, headers, and possibly content. GET /notes/42 asks for a representation; POST /notes may create one under the API contract. JSON is one possible body format, not the definition of HTTP. A successful transport exchange can still return a meaningful application error such as forbidden access or an invalid request.

Connection reuse avoids rebuilding the transport and security setup for every small request when the client and server permit it. It does not mean one connection can run infinite work without limits. Clients, proxies, and servers impose stream, connection, timeout and buffer budgets. Distinguish keep-alive or pooled connections from keeping an application session in server memory. A user session can survive a new network connection when the authentication and application state design supports that behavior.`),
 section('versions','Understand what HTTP/2 and HTTP/3 change',`HTTP/1.1 commonly reuses TCP connections, with clients often using several connections for concurrent requests. HTTP/2 introduces multiple logical streams multiplexed over a connection and compressed headers while preserving familiar HTTP semantics. With HTTP/2 over TCP, loss of earlier TCP bytes can delay delivery needed by otherwise independent HTTP streams because TCP supplies one ordered byte stream.

HTTP/3 maps HTTP onto QUIC, which uses UDP and integrates modern TLS security. QUIC provides independent reliable streams, avoiding TCP’s cross-stream loss-ordering blockage at that layer. This does not remove congestion, application serialization, shared resource limits, or every cause of waiting. A network or deployment may also require fallback to other supported versions. Choose and measure the actual browser, proxy and server path. “HTTP/3 is installed” alone does not demonstrate a latency improvement for your workload, and a new version does not change the correctness rules of saving a note.`),
 section('rpc','A remote procedure call needs a contract and a failure model',`Suppose an internal document service asks an embedding service to compute a vector. A typed procedure such as EmbedDocument can make request fields, response fields and error handling explicit. gRPC commonly generates client and server code from a service definition, uses Protocol Buffers by default, and supports unary and streaming interactions. Its standard transport model commonly uses HTTP/2. Check the specific implementation and browser gateway rather than assuming every client can use the same native stack directly.

A remote call still crosses a failure boundary. Set a deadline, propagate cancellation where supported, bound messages, and decide which failed operations may be retried. Generated code can make the call look like a local function, but the network can delay or lose a response after the remote work happened. Choosing gRPC may improve contract tooling and suit internal streaming, but does not universally make every operation faster than a well-designed HTTP/JSON interface.`),
 section('streaming','Choose a direction for ongoing updates',`Ordinary HTTP request/response is enough for saving and reading notes. A browser waiting for job progress may use repeated status requests, an HTTP event stream, or a persistent two-way channel. Server-sent events suit server-to-browser updates such as job status or generated text, while the browser can submit commands through separate HTTP requests. Reconnect support and event identifiers do not automatically supply durable replay; the application must retain and serve the relevant history.

WebSocket provides two-way message communication over a persistent connection. A common setup starts with an HTTP/1.1 upgrade handshake; other supported stacks use their specified extended connection mechanisms. It is useful when both sides send frequent interactive events, such as chat or collaborative presence. It does not supply database persistence, room authorization, duplicate prevention or application replay. Choose it for communication behavior, then design those remaining responsibilities separately.`),
 section('worked-flow','Trace one AI job through the stack',`A learner submits a document to summarize. The browser uses HTTPS to call POST /jobs with a bounded document reference and a stable operation key. The gateway validates the incoming connection and forwards an authenticated, authorized request to the API. The API saves the job and returns its identity. The browser opens an SSE progress stream while workers process the job. This avoids requiring the original POST connection to remain alive for the entire computation.

Internally, a worker may call a model service through gRPC or an HTTP API with a deadline. If the progress stream disconnects, the browser can read durable job state and reconnect under a documented event-cursor policy. If the model response times out, the worker follows its retry and billing policy rather than assuming nothing happened. Draw each connection, protocol, identity boundary and durable record. That small trace connects every acronym to a job the system actually needs.`),
 ],
 diagrams:[diagram('protocol-stack','layers','Responsibilities cooperate along one request','flowchart TB\nA[Application: read note 42 with permission] --> H[HTTP: request and response semantics]\nH --> S[TLS: protected and authenticated connection]\nS --> T[TCP: ordered reliable byte stream]\nT --> N[Network delivery between endpoints]', ['The application decides meaning and permission.','HTTP describes request and response exchange.','TLS protects the connection.','TCP carries an ordered stream.'], 'This drawing is the common HTTPS-over-TCP case. HTTP/3 instead uses QUIC, which includes security and streams over UDP.'),diagram('job-protocols','worked-flow','Different interactions can use different protocols','flowchart LR\nB[Browser] -->|HTTPS POST job| G[Gateway]\nG -->|Protected internal HTTP| A[API]\nA --> D[(Durable job)]\nD --> W[Worker]\nW -->|gRPC or HTTP with deadline| M[Model service]\nA -->|SSE status events| B', ['Create a durable job through a normal request.','Use a bounded internal model call for work.','Stream progress separately from job persistence.','Reconnect by consulting durable state.'])],
 exercise:{minutes:15,prompt:'Choose communication for three operations: save a note, stream one-way AI-job progress, and exchange frequent two-way chat events. For each, name what the protocol supplies and two things the application must still handle. Explain an HTTPS connection that terminates at a gateway.',rubric:['Match communication direction and lifetime to the operation.','Separate transport from authorization and persistence.','Explain the gateway’s separate internal hop.','Avoid claiming HTTP versions guarantee application performance.'],solution:'A normal HTTPS request/response fits save; SSE can fit one-way job updates; WebSocket can fit frequent two-way chat. Every choice still needs authorization, bounded messages, error handling and appropriate durable state. Save retries need stable identities; progress reconnect needs replay or state refresh; chat needs message persistence and room rules. TLS termination at the gateway ends the browser’s protected connection there; the gateway-to-application connection has its own protection and identity policy.'},
 questions:[choice('q1','What does TCP provide to the application?',['An ordered reliable byte stream','Exactly-once business transactions','Authorization for all users'],'An ordered reliable byte stream','Message meaning and operation correctness belong above TCP.'),choice('q2','Which protocol is a reasonable fit for one-way server progress updates over HTTP?',['Server-sent events','A process-local mutex','A database index'],'Server-sent events','SSE carries server-to-client events; persistence and replay still need application design.'),number('q3','TLS terminates at a gateway and a new protected connection goes to the API. How many connection hops must be considered?',2,'hops','Browser→gateway and gateway→API are distinct connections and trust boundaries.'),open('q4','Explain why QUIC over UDP does not imply unreliable HTTP/3 streams.','UDP itself lacks TCP’s guarantees, but QUIC implements reliable streams, congestion control and integrated security above it. Describe the complete protocol stack.'),open('q5','Why does a generated RPC client still need deadlines and retry rules?','The call crosses a network and process boundary. A response can be delayed or lost after remote work commits, so cancellation, bounded waiting and duplicate-safe retry policy remain necessary.')],
 flashcards:[['Protocol layers answer what?','Location, delivery behavior, protection, and message meaning are separate responsibilities.'],['TLS versus authorization?','TLS protects a connection and authenticates endpoints under its rules; authorization decides permitted actions.'],['HTTP/2 key change?','Multiple logical streams can share a connection while HTTP semantics remain familiar.'],['HTTP/3 transport?','QUIC supplies secure reliable streams over UDP.'],['WebSocket does not provide…','Durable history, application replay, room permissions or exactly-once business effects.']],
 mentalModel:'Name the responsibility at each layer, then trace what still needs to remain true when a connection disappears.',sources:[['MDN — Evolution of HTTP','https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP/Guides/Evolution_of_HTTP'],['IETF — HTTP/2 RFC 9113','https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9113.html'],['IETF — HTTP/3 RFC 9114','https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9114.html'],['IETF — TLS 1.3 RFC 8446','https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8446.html'],['gRPC — Core concepts and lifecycle','https://grpc.io/docs/what-is-grpc/core-concepts/'],['MDN — Server-sent events','https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Server-sent_events/Using_server-sent_events'],['IETF — WebSocket RFC 6455','https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc6455']]
});
